<article class="env-lemma" id="063C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/063C">Lemma <span data-tag="063C">31.21.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a ringed space. Let $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$. We have the following implications $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a regular sequence $\Rightarrow $ $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a Koszul-regular sequence $\Rightarrow $ $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is an $H_1$-regular sequence $\Rightarrow $ $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a quasi-regular sequence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since we may check exactness at stalks, a sequence $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ is a regular sequence if and only if the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  f_ i : \mathcal{O}_{X, x}/(f_1, \ldots , f_{i - 1}) \longrightarrow \mathcal{O}_{X, x}/(f_1, \ldots , f_{i - 1})  \]
</div>
    <p>
       are injective for all $x \in X$. The other types of regularity can be checked stalkwise as well (details omitted). Hence the implications follow from More on Algebra, Lemmas <a href="/tag/062F" data-tag="062F">15.31.2</a>, <a href="/tag/0CEM" data-tag="0CEM">15.31.3</a>, and <a href="/tag/062I" data-tag="062I">15.31.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>